 Planning Prompt (for Claude)
Goal
Plan the design and implementation of a pure Rust application that can upload and download Autonomi data given only an address, without requiring any daemon. The application must correctly handle files and directories, automatically determining what the address represents.
Core Constraints

    ✅ Use ant-core directly (daemon‑free)
    ❌ Do not depend on any local daemon or REST/gRPC gateway
    ✅ Treat all Autonomi addresses as opaque content identifiers
    ✅ The application must infer content type from downloaded data, not from the address itself

Key Architectural Principles

    The Autonomi network stores immutable byte blobs
        Addresses do not encode whether content is a file, directory, or chunk
        All higher‑level meaning is a client‑side interpretation
    Directories are a client‑side convention
        A directory is represented as:
            many independent file uploads (each with its own address)
            plus a directory manifest (structured data blob)
        The directory manifest itself is uploaded as ordinary data and has its own address
    ant-core provides only primitives
        upload bytes → address
        download address → bytes
        handling chunking, encryption, and payment
        It has no concept of files or directories

Required Download Behavior
Given an address:

    Download raw bytes using ant-core
    Attempt to parse bytes as a directory manifest (known schema + version)
    If parsing succeeds:
        treat as a directory
        create directories
        download referenced files recursively by address
    If parsing fails:
        treat content as a normal file payload
        materialize bytes directly (e.g. write to disk or stream)

There is no metadata lookup or out‑of‑band type registry.
Required Upload Behavior
When given a filesystem path:

    If the path is a file
        upload bytes directly via ant-core
        return resulting address
    If the path is a directory
        recursively walk the directory
        upload each file independently
        record:
            relative path
            file size
            file address
        build a directory manifest
        serialize deterministically (e.g. CBOR / MessagePack / JSON)
        upload the manifest via ant-core
        return the manifest address

Recommended Code Structure
Design a directory abstraction layer above ant-core:

ant-directory/
├── manifest.rs    // schema + versioning + (de)serialization
├── upload.rs      // directory → manifest → address
├── download.rs    // address → detect → materialize
├── walk.rs        // filesystem traversal
└── lib.rs

The main application depends on:

    ant-core
    this directory helper crate

Manifest Requirements

    Explicit version field (e.g. version: 1)
    Relative paths only
    Deterministic serialization
    Backward‑compatible parsing strategy

Example conceptual fields:

    path
    entry type (file / dir)
    size (files)
    address (files)

Long‑Term Alignment Strategy

    Follow the daemon‑free client behavior used by Autonomi tooling
    Do not rely on any daemon‑specific APIs or assumptions
    Keep directory logic fully deterministic and content‑driven
    Treat “directory” as one interpretation of downloaded bytes, never as a network‑level construct

What to Produce
Please produce:

    A high‑level architecture diagram or explanation
    A proposed directory manifest v1 schema
    Upload and download flow descriptions
    Error‑handling strategy for:
        unsupported manifest versions
        partial downloads
        malformed data
    Suggested public Rust API (e.g. download_any(address))

Key Design Maxim

    Everything is bytes on the network.
    Files and directories are client‑side meaning layered on top.
